<section class="wrap prose">
  <h2>Why geography games work</h2>
  <p>Recognising a country's outline, flag or neighbours is a form of spatial memory, and spatial memory strengthens with quick, repeated retrieval — exactly what a short daily game provides. Teachers use these formats as warm-ups because they are low-stakes and instantly rewarding; travellers and quiz fans use them to make the world map feel familiar. Try playing the Daily Outline every day for a month and watch your average guesses fall.</p>
  <h3>Tips for better scores</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Use the arrow.</b> In Daily Outline the direction arrow is calculated from the centre of your guess to the centre of the answer, so two guesses usually triangulate the region.</li>
    <li><b>Think in neighbours.</b> When a flag stumps you, eliminate options by region — Flag Frenzy always picks distractors from the same continent when it can.</li>
    <li><b>Distrust the Mercator map.</b> Countries near the poles look far bigger than they are. Read <a href="../guides/map-projections-explained.html">why every world map is wrong</a> before playing Bigger or Smaller.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Teachers and event organisers: want a custom branded quiz or a classroom leaderboard? <a href="../advertise.html">Talk to us about partnerships</a>.</p>
</section>
